 &: (1,2)\\leftrightarrow(3,1),\\\\\n\\text{slope }-2 &: (1,3)\\leftrightarrow(2,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nConsequently **any sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_{3}\\)**. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Non‑sunny line of the form \\(x+y=c\\)\n\n* **\\(c=4\\).**  \n  The line \\(x+y=4\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  Uncovered points: \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  Any two of these three points are either vertical, horizontal, or have slope \\(-1\\); therefore every line through a pair is non‑sunny.  \n  Since the two remaining lines must be sunny, they cannot cover these three points. Impossible.\n\n* **\\(c=3\\).**  \n  The line \\(x+y=3\\) contains \\((1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  Uncovered points: \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  By Lemma 1 the only sunny pair among them is \\((1,1)\\) with \\((2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \n  Use a sunny line \\(y=x\\) to cover this pair.  \n  The two points \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) remain; the line through them has slope \\(-1\\) and is non‑sunny.  \n  Hence the two sunny lines cannot finish the covering. Impossible.\n\n* **\\(c=2\\).**  \n  The line \\(x+y=2\\) contains only \\((1,1)\\).  \n  Five points remain uncovered. By Lemma 1 each sunny line can cover at most two points, so two sunny lines can cover at most four points – insufficient. Impossible.\n\nThus no diagonal non‑sunny line works.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Non‑sunny horizontal line \\(y=d\\)\n\n* **\\(d=1\\).**  \n  The line \\(y=1\\) contains \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \n  Uncovered points: \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\).  \n  Any two of these three points are vertical or have slope \\(-1\\); consequently every line through a pair is non‑sunny.  \n  The two sunny lines cannot cover them. Impossible.\n\n* **\\(d=2\\).**  \n  The line \\(y=2\\) contains \\((1,2),(2,2)\\).  \n  Uncovered points: \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \n  The only sunny pair