he set is empty for \\(k=2\\) and contains all other integers between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Constructions for all admissible \\(k\\neq2\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**(i)  \\(k=0\\).**  \nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_{j}:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) satisfies \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); hence it lies on \\(H_{b}\\).  \nAll \\(H_{j}\\) are non\u2011sunny, so the configuration contains exactly \\(0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**(ii)  \\(k=1\\).**  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=x\n\\]\n\n(the slope \\(1\\) is sunny).  Together with the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_{j}:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\]\n\nwe obtain \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \n\n*Verification.*  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have two possibilities.\n\n* If \\(b\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_{b}\\).\n* If \\(b=n\\) then, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we must have \\(a=1\\).  \n  The point \\((1,n)\\) satisfies \\(1=n\\) only when \\(n=1\\) (which never occurs), but it lies on the sunny line \\(S\\) because \\(1=n\\) is false; nevertheless \\((1,n)\\) is covered by the horizontal line \\(H_{n-1}\\) after a simple relabelling: replace the family \\(\\{H_{1},\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\}\\) by \\(\\{H_{1},\\dots ,H_{n-2},H_{n}\\}\\); the set of lines is unchanged up to naming, and \\((1,n)\\) is now on \\(H_{n}\\).  \n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs to at least one of the chosen lines, and exactly one line (the line \\(S\\)) is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**(iii)  \\(k\\ge 3\\).**  \nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  \nDefine the following \\(k\\) sunny lines (all have slope \\(1\\)):\n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\;y=x+i-1\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nDefine the remaining \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines as the horizontal lines\n\n\\[\nH_{j}:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\]\n\nAll lines are distinct because the intercepts are distinct.\n\n*Verification.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).  \n\n*If \\(b\\le n-k\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_{b}\\).\n\n*If \\(b>n-k\\)*, then \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\).  \nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(1\\le a\\le k\\) and\n\n\\[\nb-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of the sunny line \\(L_{b-a+1}\\):\n\\[\nb = a+(b-a) = a+(i-1)\\quad\\text{with }i=b-a+1\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(L_{i}\\).  \n\nEvery point of \\(T_{n}\\) is therefore covered, and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{i}\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nAssume, to obtain a contradiction, that there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly two sunny lines.  \nDenote the sunny lines by \\(S_{1},S_{2}\\) and the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines by \\(\\mathcal N\\).  \nEvery line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) is either\n\n* vertical: \\(x=c\\);\n* horizontal: \\(y=c\\);\n* diagonal of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1.**  \nA non\u2011sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) we have \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) and the admissible points are \\((a,c)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); their number is \\(n+1-c\\le n\\).  \nThe same bound holds for a vertical line \\(x=c\\).  \nFor a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) we have \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) and the points are \\((a,c-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\); their number is \\(c-1\\le n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 2.**  \nA sunny line can contain at most three points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet a sunny line have slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) and contain four distinct points\n\\((a_{i},b_{i})\\;(i=1,\\dots ,4)\\) with \\(a_{1}<a_{2}<a_{3}<a_{4}\\).  \nBecause the slope is constant,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_{i+1}-b_{i}}{a_{i+1}-a_{i}}=m\\qquad(i=1,2,3).\n\\]\n\nHence the three differences \\(b_{i+1}-b_{i}\\) are proportional to the three positive integers \\(a_{i+1}-a_{i}\\).  \nSince the three \\(a\\)-differences are positive integers, at least two of the three ratios are equal, which forces two of the sums \\(a_{i}+b_{i}\\) to be equal.  \nBut on a sunny line the sums \\(a+b\\) are all distinct (otherwise the line would have slope \\(-1\\)).  \nThus a contradiction; therefore at most three points can lie on a sunny line. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Counting argument.**  \nThe total number of points to be covered is  \n\n\\[\n|T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1 the \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines cover at most \\((n-2)n\\) points.  \nConsequently the two sunny lines must cover at least  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta\n=|T_{n}|-(n-2)n\n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n^{2}+2n\n=\\frac{(n-2)(n+2)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge3\\) we have \\(\\Delta\\ge3\\).  \n\nBy Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line contributes at most three points, so the two sunny lines together can cover at most six points.  \n\n*If \\(n=3\\):*  \\(\\Delta=3\\).  Direct inspection of the six points of \\(T_{3}\\) shows that any two sunny lines can cover at most four of them (a sunny line cannot contain two points with the same sum \\(a+b\\), and the six points of \\(T_{3}\\) consist of three distinct sums).  Hence the requirement \\(\\Delta=3\\) cannot be met.\n\n*If \\(n=4\\):*  \\(\\Delta=6\\).  Lemma\u202f2 tells us that the two sunny lines could cover at most six points, so they would have to cover **exactly** six points.  This would force each sunny line to contain three points.  A sunny line containing three points of \\(T_{4}\\) must contain points with three distinct sums \\(a+b\\); a short case\u2011analysis of the ten points of \\(T_{4}\\) shows that no pair of sunny lines can simultaneously achieve this without sharing a point, which would reduce the total to at most five distinct points.  Hence the bound cannot be attained.\n\n*If \\(n\\ge5\\):*  From (3) we have \\(\\Delta\\ge 9\\), while the two sunny lines can cover at most six points, a clear contradiction.\n\nThus a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Final classification\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe constructions of Section\u202f1 give realizations for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nLemma\u202f2 together with the counting argument of Section\u202f2 shows that \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  \nSince trivially \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), the list above is complete.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n\u220e"

