 of points covered by the non\u2011sunny family, so the three points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_1=(1,n-1),\\qquad P_2=(2,n-1),\\qquad P_3=(1,n)\n   \\]\n\n   (the only points of \\(T_n\\) not lying on those horizontals) must still be covered by the two sunny lines.\n\n   *Observation:* a sunny line cannot contain two points whose coordinates sum to the same value, because equal sums give slope \\(-1\\), which is forbidden.  \n   The sums are  \n\n   \\[\n   P_1:\\;1+(n-1)=n,\\qquad\n   P_2:\\;2+(n-1)=n+1,\\qquad\n   P_3:\\;1+n=n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(P_2\\) and \\(P_3\\) lie on the same diagonal \\(a+b=n+1\\) and cannot be together on a sunny line.  \n   Moreover \\(P_1\\) shares its \\(y\\)-coordinate with \\(P_2\\) (so a horizontal line would be needed) and its \\(x\\)-coordinate with \\(P_3\\) (so a vertical line would be needed); both are non\u2011sunny. Consequently each of the three points requires its own sunny line, contradicting the assumption that only two sunny lines are available.  \n   Therefore **no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).**\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   All non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable **except** \\(k=2\\).  \n   Hence the complete answer is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\,}.\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Constructions for every admissible \\(k\\neq2\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 1.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_{j}:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(H_{b}\\).  \nAll lines are non\u2011sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is realised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 1.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nUse the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_{j}:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\]\n\nand the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=2x+n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThe horizontals cover every point with \\(b\\le n-1\\).  \nThe only point of \\(T_{n}\\) not covered by them is \\((1,n)\\); substituting \\(a=1\\) into the equation of \\(S\\) gives \\(y=2\\cdot1+n-2=n\\), so \\((1,n)\\in S\\).  \nAll \\(n\\) lines are distinct and exactly one of them (the line \\(S\\)) is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 1.3  \\(3\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\;y=x+i-1\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k) \\tag{sunny lines}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nV_{j}:\\;x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k) \\tag{vertical, non\u2011sunny lines}.\n\\]\n\nAll \\(L_{i}\\) have slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); all \\(V_{j}\\) are vertical, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nThe total number of lines is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\).\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\).\n\n*If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\)*, then \\(b-a=i-1\\) for a unique \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) and \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_{i}\\).\n\n*If \\(b-a\\ge k\\)*, then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2a\\le n+1-k\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\le n-1\\) we have \\(\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-k\\); consequently \\(a\\le n-k\\) and the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\) (which exists because \\(1\\le a\\le n-k\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the chosen lines, and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{i}\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 1.4  \\(k=n\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines \\(L_{i}\\;(i=1,\\dots ,n-1)\\) defined as in 1.3 and add the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=2x+n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThe \\(L_{i}\\) cover all points whose difference \\(b-a\\) belongs to \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).  \nFor a point with \\(b-a=n-1\\) we have \\(a\\le1\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)), hence the only such point is \\((1,n)\\); it lies on \\(S\\) because \\(n=2\\cdot1+n-2\\).  \nAll \\(n\\) lines are sunny and distinct, giving a configuration with \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Impossibility of exactly two sunny lines\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge3\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly two sunny lines.\n\nReplace every non\u2011sunny line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) by a horizontal line; this can only **increase** the set of points covered by the non\u2011sunny part, because any horizontal line \\(y=c\\) contains at least as many points of \\(T_{n}\\) as any other line of the same family (vertical or diagonal).  \nHence we may assume, without loss of generality, that the non\u2011sunny lines are precisely\n\n\\[\nH_{j}:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-2).\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-2\\) horizontals cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\) with second coordinate \\(b\\le n-2\\).  \nThe three points that are **not** covered are\n\n\\[\nP_{1}=(1,n-1),\\qquad P_{2}=(2,n-1),\\qquad P_{3}=(1,n).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the two sunny lines must cover the set \\(\\{P_{1},P_{2},P_{3}\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Lemma.** A sunny line cannot contain two points of \\(T_{n}\\) whose coordinates sum to the same integer.\n\n*Proof.* If \\((a_{1},b_{1})\\) and \\((a_{2},b_{2})\\) satisfy \\(a_{1}+b_{1}=a_{2}+b_{2}\\) and are distinct, then  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b_{2}-b_{1}}{a_{2}-a_{1}}=\n\\frac{-(a_{2}-a_{1})}{a_{2}-a_{1}}=-1,\n\\]\n\nso the line through them has slope \\(-1\\), which is prohibited for a sunny line. \u220e\n\nApplying the Lemma to the three points (1) we compute their sums:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ns(P_{1}) &= 1+(n-1)=n,\\\\\ns(P_{2}) &= 2+(n-1)=n+1,\\\\\ns(P_{3}) &= 1+n=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(P_{2}\\) and \\(P_{3}\\) lie on the same diagonal \\(a+b=n+1\\) and cannot belong to a common sunny line.  \nMoreover \\(P_{1}\\) shares its \\(y\\)-coordinate with \\(P_{2}\\) (so a horizontal line would be needed) and its \\(x\\)-coordinate with \\(P_{3}\\) (so a vertical line would be needed); both types are non\u2011sunny. Consequently each of the three points requires a distinct sunny line.\n\nThis contradicts the assumption that only two sunny lines are available. Hence **no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists** for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Final classification\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe constructions of Sections\u202f1.1\u20131.4 realise every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nand Section\u202f2 proves that \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  \nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\n\u220e"

