rtical lines.  \n     → This part is also correct.\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)**  \n\n   The solution argues:\n\n   - “The diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) must be one of the lines (otherwise its \\(n\\) points would need at least \\(n\\) other lines).”  \n     *This observation is correct*: any line not equal to this diagonal can intersect it in at most one point, so covering its \\(n\\) points would require at least \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n   - “After removing that diagonal, the two right‑most columns contain the three points \\((n-1,1), (n-1,2), (n,1)\\). Any line through two of them is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently a sunny line can contain at most one of these three points. With only two sunny lines we can cover at most two of the three points – a contradiction.”  \n\n     *Critical analysis:*  \n     • The diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) **does** contain the points \\((n-1,2)\\) and \\((n,1)\\). Hence after we have taken this diagonal as one of the \\(n\\) lines, those two points are already covered.  \n     • The only uncovered point among the three listed is \\((n-1,1)\\). This single point can be covered either by a sunny line or by a non‑sunny line (e.g. the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\)).  \n     • Therefore the statement “a sunny line can contain at most one of these three points” is irrelevant to the covering problem, and the conclusion that two sunny lines cannot suffice is unfounded.  \n\n     → **Critical Error:** The impossibility proof for \\(k=2\\) is logically invalid; it does not rule out the existence of a configuration with exactly two sunny lines.\n\n4. **Construction for every \\(k\\ge3\\)**  \n\n   The solution proceeds as follows:\n\n   - **Vertical lines (1).**  \n     It introduces the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  \n     *Verification:* These lines are non‑sunny and indeed cover all points with abscissa \\(\\le n-k\\).\n\n   - **Definition of the right‑hand triangle \\(T_k\\)