(n\\ge3\\).\n\n4. **Construction for every \\(k\\;(3\\le k\\le n)\\).**  \n\n   *Step 1 – non‑sunny part.*  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   They cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – the right‑hand triangle.*  \n   The remaining points form the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\;\\Big|\\;n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\Bigr\\},\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   a triangular Ferrers diagram of size \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – sunny lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; \\text{the line through }(n-i+1,1)\\text{ and }(n,i).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{i-1}{i}\\), which is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n   For \\(i=1\\) we take any sunny line through \\((n,1)\\); we choose  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\;y=2(x-n)+1\\qquad(\\text{slope }2).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are distinct (different slopes or different\n   intercepts) and none of them is vertical, so they are all different from the vertical lines (1).\n\n   *Step 4 – point‑by‑point verification for \\(T_k\\).*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\).  Set  \n\n   \\[\n   i:=n-a+b\\qquad(\\text{so }1\\le i\\le k).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(i=n-a+b\\ge n-a+(1)=n-a+1\\ge1\\), and because\n   \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) we have \\(i\\le k\\).  \n   Write \\(t:=b-1\\;(0\\le t\\le i-1)\\).  Then  \n\n   \\[\n   a=n-i+1+t,\\qquad b=1+t .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line through \\((n-i+1,1)\\) and \\((n,i)\\), i.e. on \\(\\ell_i\\) (for \\(i\\ge2\\));\n   when \\(i=1\\) the point is \\((n,1)\\) and belongs to \\(\\ell_1\\) by construction.\n   Consequently every point of \\(T_k\\) is covered by one of the sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).\n\n   *Step 5 – completeness