            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###**  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non‑sunny** if it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(y=c\\)), the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(x=c\\)) or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \nAll other lines are **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The cases \\(k=0,1,2\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n  \\]\n\n  are all non‑sunny.  For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{a+b}\\).  Thus the condition is satisfied with \\(k=0\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Put  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &\\mathcal V=\\{x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\},\\\\\n  &\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=n+1\\},\\\\\n  &\\mathcal H=\\{y=1\\}.\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  The lines in \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\cup\\mathcal H\\) are non‑sunny; there are \\((n-2)+1+1=n\\) of them.  \n  The vertical lines cover all points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-2\\).  \n  The diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) covers the two points \\((n,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\).  \n  The horizontal line \\(y=1\\) covers the remaining point \\((n-1,1)\\).  \n  Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of these lines, and the number of sunny lines is \\(0\\).  \n  To obtain exactly one sunny line we replace any one of the non‑sunny lines (for instance \\(y=1\\)) by a sunny line, e.g. \\(y=2x+1\\).  The new family still contains \\(n\\) distinct lines and now has precisely one sunny line.\n\n* **\\(k=2\\).**  \n  Define  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &\\mathcal V=\\{x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\quad(\\text{empty if }n=3),\\\\\n  &\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=n+1\\},\\\\\n  &\\mathcal S_1=\\{y=2x+1\\},\\qquad\n  &\\mathcal S_2=\\{y=3x+2\\}.\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  The set \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\) consists of \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines; \\(\\mathcal S_1,\\mathcal S_2\\) are sunny and distinct.  \n  As in the previous case, the vertical lines cover all points with first coordinate \\(\\le n-3\\); the diagonal covers \\((n,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\).  \n  The only point of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered is \\((n-1,1)\\); it lies on the sunny line \\(\\mathcal S_1\\).  Hence all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and exactly two lines are sunny.\n\nThus the values \\(k=0,1,2\\) are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Inductive step for \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nAssume that for every integer \\(n'\\) with \\(3\\le n'<n\\) and every \\(k'\\) with \\(0\\le k'\\le n'\\) a suitable family of \\(n'\\) lines exists.  \nFix a number \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the required number of non‑sunny lines}).\n\\]\n\n**Non‑sunny part.**  \nTake  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=i\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,m-1\\}\\quad(\\text{empty if }m\\le 1),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(m\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le m-1\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\); every point with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lies on \\(\\mathcal D\\).\n\n**Remaining points.**  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nS:=T_n\\setminus\\bigl(\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\le m-1\\}\\cup\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in S\\) then \\(a\\ge m\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\).  Write  \n\n\\[\na=m+a',\\qquad b=b',\\qquad a',b'\\ge 1.\n\\]\n\nThe inequality \\(a+b\\le n=m+k\\) becomes \\(a'+b'\\le k\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(m+a',b')\\mid a',b'\\ge 1,\\;a'+b'\\le k\\}= \\{m\\}\\times T_{k-1}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(|S|=|T_{k-1}|=\\dfrac{(k-1)k}{2}\\).\n\n**Sunny part.**  \nBy the induction hypothesis applied to the parameter \\(k-1\\) (note that \\(k-1\\le n-1\\)), the set \\(T_{k-1}\\) can be covered by \\(k-1\\) distinct sunny lines.  Translating each of those lines by the vector \\((m,0)\\) yields \\(k-1\\) distinct sunny lines that cover the set \\(S\\).  \n\nAdd one further sunny line that is not parallel to any of the already used lines (for instance \\(y=2x+1\\)).  The total number of sunny lines is therefore exactly  \n\n\\[\n(k-1)+1=k .\n\\]\n\n**Counting lines.**  \nThe construction uses  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(m-1)}_{\\text{vertical}}+ \\underbrace{1}_{\\text{diagonal}}+ \\underbrace{(k-1)}_{\\text{sunny covering }S}+ \\underbrace{1}_{\\text{extra sunny}} = m-1+1+k-1+1 = m+k = n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\nThus for the given \\(k\\) we have produced a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Completion of the induction\n\nThe base cases \\(k=0,1,2\\) have been settled in Section 1, and the inductive step shows that if the statement holds for all parameters smaller than \\(n\\) then it also holds for every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  By ordinary induction on \\(n\\) (starting from \\(n=3\\)) the statement is true for all \\(n\\ge 3\\) and all \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\)\n\nBy definition a “number of sunny lines’’ cannot exceed the total number of lines, and it cannot be negative.  Hence any admissible \\(k\\) must satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  The constructions above show that **every** integer in this interval is attainable.  Therefore the set of all possible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
